  "context": "Published in Hamburg in 1867, Volume 1 of Das Kapital is Karl Marx's foundational critique of political economy. It provides a detailed theoretical and historical examination of how capital extracts surplus value from wage labor, commodifies social relations, and drives systemic capitalist accumulation.",
  "relatesToToday": "Marx's analysis of commodity fetishism, automated factory production, lengthening of the working day, and primitive accumulation remains vital for understanding modern platform capitalism, worker exploitation, global supply chains, and wealth inequality.",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "The dual nature of commodities (use-value and exchange-value) stems from the dual character of human labor.",
    "Surplus value is generated when workers produce more value than the cost of their labor-power (wages).",
    "Capitalist accumulation drives systemic consolidation, technological displacement of labor, and periodic economic crises."
